ty of Karlowitz returns almost all of Hungary to Habsburg rule</title></g><g class="rf-ev"><line x1="504.3" x2="504.3" y1="160" y2="166"/><circle cx="504.3" cy="160" r="8"/><text x="504.3" y="164" text-anchor="middle">6</text><title>1713: Charles VI issues the Pragmatic Sanction, making the lands indivisible</title></g><g class="rf-ev"><line x1="680.4" x2="680.4" y1="160" y2="166"/><circle cx="680.4" cy="160" r="8"/><text x="680.4" y="164" text-anchor="middle">7</text><title>1781: Joseph II issues the Patent of Toleration and abolishes personal serfdom</title></g><g class="rf-ev"><line x1="740.0" x2="740.0" y1="160" y2="166"/><circle cx="740.0" cy="160" r="8"/><text x="740.0" y="164" text-anchor="middle">8</text><title>1804: Francis II proclaims himself hereditary Emperor of Austria</title></g><text class="rf-axis" x="20" y="200">1526 CE</text><text class="rf-axis" x="740" y="200" text-anchor="end">1804 CE</text></svg><figcaption>Relative power on the Institute's 0–10 scale (each profile scaled to its own peak). Numbers mark the key events below.</figcaption></figure><ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1526</span><span class="rf-text">Louis II dies at Mohács; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-holy-roman-emperor">Ferdinand of Austria</a> is elected king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1529</span><span class="rf-text">First Ottoman siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> is repulsed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1620</span><span class="rf-text">Bohemian revolt crushed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-white-mountain">Battle of White Mountain</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1683</span><span class="rf-text">Second Ottoman siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> broken at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/b