    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-holy-roman-emperor"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-holy-roman-emperor.jpg" alt="Ferdinand I, Holy Roman Emperor" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-holy-roman-emperor">Ferdinand I, Holy Roman Emperor</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hans_Bocksberger_der_Aeltere_001(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hans Bocksberger the Elder, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Habsburg Monarchy was born of a dynastic accident on the battlefield. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-holy-roman-emperor">Archduke Ferdinand of Austria</a>, younger brother of the Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, had received the Austrian hereditary lands in the partition agreements of Worms (1521) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> (1522), and in 1521 he married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anna-of-bohemia-and-hungary">Anna of Bohemia and Hungary</a>, while his sister Mary married her brother, King Louis II. When Louis drowned in flight from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mohacs">Battle of Mohács</a> on 29 August 1526, Ferdinand claimed both his crowns. The Bohemian estates elected him king in October 1526, bringing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, Silesia and the two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatias</a> under Habsburg rule. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> was contested: one diet chose the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnate</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-zapolya">John Zápolya</a> in November, and a rival diet at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Pozsony</a> elected Ferdinand in December. The Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent">Suleiman the Magnificent</a> backed Zápolya, besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1529 and occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a> in 1541, leaving Hungary divided between a Habsburg ‘<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-hungary">Royal Hungary</a>’ in the north and west, an Ottoman province in the centre and an autonomous Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> in the east. The new union therefore began as a defensive alliance of very different realms, held together by one ruler and by fear of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. On Ferdinand’s death in 1564 the lands were divided among his three sons, and they were only fully reunited under Leopold I in 1665.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kutna-hora"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kutna-hora.jpg" alt="Kutná Hora" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kutna-hora">Kutná Hora</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kutn%C3%A1_Hora_(003).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">al.trcka, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The monarchy was overwhelmingly agrarian. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> large noble estates relied increasingly on the forced labour of peasants, the robot, a pattern often called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-serfdom">second serfdom</a>, while the Alpine lands kept a freer peasantry. Mining brought exceptional revenues: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutna-hora">Kutná Hora</a> and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jachymov">Joachimsthal</a> (Jáchymov), whose coins gave the thaler and eventually the dollar its name, mercury from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idrija">Idrija</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and gold from the mining towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-hungary">Upper Hungary</a>, and salt from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzkammergut">Salzkammergut</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pest-hungary">Pest</a> grew as court, administrative and commercial centres, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-vi-holy-roman-emperor">Charles VI</a> declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trieste">Trieste</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rijeka">Fiume</a> free ports in 1719 to open an outlet to the Adriatic. Religion shaped society profoundly. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a> spread widely in the sixteenth century, but after 1620 the dynasty enforced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> in Bohemia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, confiscated rebel estates and drove tens of thousands of Protestants into exile, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> took over schools and universities. Hungary and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>, by contrast, retained substantial Calvinist and Lutheran communities. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a> produced a pervasive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> piety of pilgrimages, monasteries and brotherhoods. In the eighteenth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a>’s Urbarium of 1767 regulated Hungarian peasant obligations, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Joseph II</a> abolished personal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> in the Bohemian lands in 1781 and in Austria in 1782, though labour dues on the land remained.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-white-mountain"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-white-mountain.jpg" alt="Battle of White Mountain" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-white-mountain">Battle of White Mountain</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Battle_of_White_Mountain_(by_Peter_Snayers).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Peter Snayers, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>War with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> dominated the monarchy’s first two centuries. After surviving the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1529, the Habsburgs built the Military Frontier, a fortified belt along the Croatian and Hungarian border settled by soldier-farmers, many of them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serb</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlachs">Vlach</a> refugees, and directed after 1556 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aulic-war-council">Aulic War Council</a> (Hofkriegsrat) in Vienna. The inconclusive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-turkish-war">Long Turkish War</a> of 1593–1606 ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-zsitvatorok">Peace of Zsitvatorok</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-tyrants">the Thirty</a> Years’ War the Bohemian revolt was crushed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-white-mountain">Battle of White Mountain</a> on 8 November 1620, and imperial armies raised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albrecht-von-wallenstein">Albrecht von Wallenstein</a> fought across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> in 1648. The decisive turn came in 1683, when a relief army led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-iii-sobieski">John III Sobieski</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> broke the second Ottoman siege of Vienna at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-vienna-1683">Battle of Kahlenberg</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-league-1684">Holy League</a> retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a> in 1686, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-eugene-of-savoy">Prince Eugene of Savoy</a> destroyed the Ottoman army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-zenta">Battle of Zenta</a> in 1697, leading to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-karlowitz">Treaty of Karlowitz</a> in 1699. Eugene shared victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-blenheim">Blenheim</a> in 1704 and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> in 1717. The monarchy then lost Silesia to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick II of Prussia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-austrian-succession">War of the Austrian Succession</a>, failed to recover it in the Seven Years’ War despite victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kolin">Kolín</a> in 1757, and suffered repeated defeats by revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> after 1792.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-vi-holy-roman-emperor"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-vi-holy-roman-emperor.jpg" alt="Charles VI, Holy Roman Emperor" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-vi-holy-roman-emperor">Charles VI, Holy Roman Emperor</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johann_Gottfried_Auerbach_004.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Johann Gottfried Auerbach, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Habsburg Monarchy was never a single state with one constitution. Each kingdom and duchy kept its own estates, diet, laws and nobility, and the ruler was separately archduke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>; from 1558 the head of the house almost always also held the elective title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a>. Ferdinand I created central bodies in 1527, a Privy Council, a Court Chancellery and a Court Chamber for finance, but these sat on top of provincial institutions rather than replacing them. After 1620 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renewed-land-ordinance">Renewed Land Ordinance</a> of 1627 made the Bohemian crown hereditary and weakened its estates, while Hungary’s diet retained wide powers and in 1687 accepted hereditary Habsburg succession in the male line. To prevent partition, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-vi-holy-roman-emperor">Charles VI</a> issued the Pragmatic Sanction of 1713, declaring the lands indivisible and allowing a daughter to inherit; he spent years securing its acceptance at home and abroad. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a> (r. 1740–1780) and her minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-wilhelm-von-haugwitz">Friedrich Wilhelm von Haugwitz</a> merged the Austrian and Bohemian chancelleries, created a permanent tax-funded army and a Council of State in 1760, and introduced compulsory elementary schooling in 1774. Her son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Joseph II</a> pushed further, with the Patent of Toleration of 1781, the dissolution of contemplative monasteries and German as the language of administration, provoking revolts in Hungary and the Austrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/karlskirche"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karlskirche.jpg" alt="Karlskirche" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/karlskirche">Karlskirche</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karlskirche_Abendsonne_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Thomas Ledl, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For contemporaries the monarchy was above all the bulwark of Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, and its reconquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> after 1683 redrew the map of south-eastern Europe, opening the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> basin to resettlement by German, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serb</a>, Romanian and other colonists whose descendants shaped the region’s ethnic mosaic. Its Catholic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a> set the religious character of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and much of Hungary for centuries. The Habsburg court made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> a cultural capital: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> architecture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-bernhard-fischer-von-erlach">Johann Bernhard Fischer von Erlach</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-lukas-von-hildebrandt">Johann Lukas von Hildebrandt</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlskirche">Karlskirche</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belvedere-vienna">Belvedere</a> and the abbey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melk">Melk</a> defined a Central European style, and in the later eighteenth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christoph-willibald-gluck">Christoph Willibald Gluck</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-haydn">Joseph Haydn</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfgang-amadeus-mozart">Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart</a> created the Viennese Classical style in music. The Theresian and Josephine reforms became a model of enlightened absolutism, and the bureaucratic tradition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephinism">Josephinism</a>, with its educated, state-minded civil service, outlived its founder. The monarchy also took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-partition-of-poland">First Partition of Poland</a> in 1772, acquiring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, and gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukovina">Bukovina</a> in 1775. Its central problem, how to govern many peoples with distinct laws and languages under one dynasty, passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>, and still informs debates about federalism and nationality in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-marengo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-marengo.jpg" alt="Battle of Marengo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-marengo">Battle of Marengo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lejeune_-_Bataille_de_Marengo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Louis-François, Baron Lejeune, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Habsburg Monarchy did not collapse in 1804; it was renamed and reorganised. The wars against revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from 1792 cost it heavily. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-campo-formio">Treaty of Campo Formio</a> in 1797 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Francis II</a> gave up the Austrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> in return for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, and after defeats at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-marengo">Marengo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hohenlinden">Hohenlinden</a> in 1800 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-luneville">Treaty of Lunéville</a> of 1801 confirmed French control of the left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and set in motion the secularisation of the German church states, which undermined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> on which Habsburg prestige rested. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a> proclaimed himself Emperor of the French in May 1804, Francis feared that the elective imperial title might pass from his house and that he would rank below the Corsican and the Russian tsar. By a patent of 11 August 1804 he declared himself hereditary Emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, as Francis I, giving all his lands a single imperial title while leaving the rights of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and the other kingdoms formally intact. After the defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-austerlitz">Austerlitz</a> and the formation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a>, he dissolved the Holy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> on 6 August 1806. The monarchy continued as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a>, became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Dual Monarchy</a> of Austria-Hungary in 1867 and broke apart into successor states in 1918.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Habsburg Monarchy united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> under one dynasty for nearly three centuries, halted Ottoman expansion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>, imposed the Catholic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a>, produced Viennese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> and Classical culture and pioneered enlightened absolutism, before becoming the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a> in 1804.</em></p>

